            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>Enterprise-scale overseas advertising fund management must answer three questions: Is there enough money, is it being spent too quickly, and is spending aligned with the plan? Account balance shows the current fund position, but it does not reveal budget execution, account health, or financial risk. The following metrics provide a more practical operating baseline for media and finance teams.</p>
<h2>Which metrics matter most for fund management?</h2>
<h3>1. Why can an account run out of funds even when its balance looks sufficient?\n</h3>
<p><strong>Track available-balance coverage days, not only the balance amount.</strong></p>
<p>Available-balance coverage = available balance ÷ average daily spend over the last seven days</p>
<p>For example, an account with CNY 200,000 in available funds and CNY 50,000 in average daily spend has only four days of coverage and remains exposed to delivery interruption. If a launch, product update, or promotion is expected to raise spending, coverage should be recalculated using the projected daily spend.</p>
<p>Companies should also use tiered alerts. Coverage above seven days is generally normal; three to seven days requires recharge planning; less than three days needs immediate action. However, high-spend accounts should not automatically be given large balances. The real question is whether funds support a clear 14-to-30-day media plan.</p>
<h3>2. Why do recharge amounts and advertising spend often fail to match?\n</h3>
<p><strong>Separate fund transactions from delivery spend.</strong> Recharges enter advertising accounts, while spend is generated by impressions, clicks, or other billable events on the platform. The two amounts therefore do not normally settle at the same time or in equal amounts.</p>
<p>Reconciliation should compare each advertising account, currency, and billing period. Key items include platform invoices that have not yet been received, date shifts caused by time zones, payment fees and taxes, grants or refunds, and funds still in transit through the payment provider.</p>
<p>A clear judgment standard is necessary: explainable differences should remain in a temporary adjustment area, while unmatched items that exceed the agreed settlement window should be escalated. Otherwise, normal timing differences may obscure genuine financial issues.</p>
<h2>Which metrics reveal budget control problems before delivery is disrupted?</h2>
<h3>3. Does high daily spend mean the campaign is performing well?</h3>
<p><strong>Not necessarily. Daily spend measures pacing, not performance or control.</strong> Monitor four metrics together:</p>
<ul>
<li>Budget variance rate = (actual spend − planned spend) ÷ planned spend</li>
<li>Account utilization rate = spend ÷ recharged available funds</li>
<li>Single-account spend share = account spend ÷ total spend</li>
<li>Number of consecutive budget overruns</li>
</ul>
<p>Consider an anonymous utility app running a two-week campaign. Total spending remained within budget, but two core accounts reached their daily caps two days early. The total plan appeared healthy, while the individual accounts had already lost room to adjust. Budget variance should therefore be analyzed at account and campaign levels, not only at the total level.</p>
<h3>4. How can multiple advertising accounts prevent excessive fund concentration?\n</h3>
<p><strong>Apply account-level spending caps rather than distributing budgets automatically.</strong> When accounts are split by region, product line, creative strategy, or platform, monitor the spending share of the top three accounts. If one account consumes more than 60% of the total budget without a clear case for scaling, pause automated budget increases and investigate creative fatigue, audience saturation, and attribution duplication.</p>
<p>Budgets should not necessarily be divided equally. A high-performing account may receive more funding if its recent conversions are stable, tracking is reliable, and inventory remains sufficient. Equal allocation may appear fair while suppressing winning accounts and subsidizing inefficient ones.</p>
<h3>5. When should balance and risk alerts be triggered?\n</h3>
<p><strong>Alerts should consider amount, time, and burn rate together.</strong> A low balance does not always mean high risk. CNY 100,000 with CNY 10,000 in daily spend may cover ten days, while the same balance with CNY 80,000 in daily spend may stop delivery almost immediately.</p>
<p>Risk controls should also monitor unusual recharge frequency, repeated budget changes, sudden spending increases, dependence on a single payment method, and changes in the login environments of multiple accounts. Unplanned recharges, spend growth above 50% within 24 hours, or identical anomalies across several accounts should trigger joint review by the media lead and finance team. The correct response is not automatically to add more funds.</p>
<h2>How should these metrics become an actionable fund dashboard?\n</h2>
<p>A three-level operating rhythm works well:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Daily dashboard:</strong> Available balance, projected depletion date, spend over the last 24 hours, budget variance, and account concentration.</li>
<li><strong>Weekly review:</strong> Match recharge plans with future media plans, and examine high-spend accounts, low-spend accounts, and abnormal fluctuations.</li>
<li><strong>Monthly settlement:</strong> Reconcile invoices by account, currency, platform, and billing period. Investigate and resolve differences before processing refunds or adjustments.</li>
</ol>
<p>Teams should also standardize balance definitions by separating total balance, available balance, pending amounts, and incurred-but-not-yet-deducted spend. Time zones, currencies, and exchange-rate rules must be consistent. When media and finance teams use the same data, fund alerts become actionable controls rather than routine notifications.</p>
